---
// /dweck-sukkot-picks — an unlisted page, not linked anywhere on the site.
// A handful of 30-90 second clips pulled out of Rabbi Dweck's own Sukkot shiurim
// (full talks + a permanent public audio_url ship in his RJD-app transcript
// archive), so he can listen and mark, from any device, which ones he'd want
// used. Picks persist in Supabase via /api/sukkot-picks and are visible next
// time the page loads, for him or for Matthew.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Sukkot Clips for Rabbi Dweck';
const pageDescription = 'A shortlist of short Sukkot clips — tap the ones you like.';

const clips = [
  {
    id: 'walking-in-circles',
    title: 'Walking in Circles',
    source: 'Our Internal Transience and the Fear of God',
    audio: '/audio/dweck-sukkot/walking-in-circles.mp3',
    duration: '1:17',
    transcript: `Perhaps the most predominant theme is the fact that it is a Chag that celebrates transience — the fact that things are not permanent. So we sit in this, and halakhically it needs to be something that isn't permanent, because if it's permanent, it's no longer a Sukkah. And we have to pour our hearts into something that is going away.

Chazal say, teshvu k'ein taduru — that you have to live in this place as though it is your abode, this is the place that you live — and you're to pour your heart into it, even though you're going to take it down seven days later. Some of us wait till Hanukkah to take it down. But it's supposed to be: we're not in it anymore, right?

Not only that, but we do things that seem on the surface to be also futile. We literally walk around in circles every day. Right? I mean, when we say that in English — "you're walking around in circles" — it's not a good thing. Tomorrow we will walk around in circles seven times. Around, around. We take these branches — you know, glorified lemon — and walk around in circles with these things.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'hillel-bath',
    title: "Hillel's Bath",
    source: 'How Succot Teaches Happiness',
    audio: '/audio/dweck-sukkot/hillel-bath.mp3',
    duration: '0:53',
    transcript: `This is Hillel HaZaken's teaching — very famous, everybody knows it, but they don't realize it's Hillel HaZaken who says: if I'm not going to take care of myself, if I'm not going to take care of my own life, who will? Nobody will. Our life, as we have it, leading it, is all that we really have.

And so he's the same one who says, "I'm going to go do this huge mitzvah." And the Talmud says, what are you going to do? And he says, "I'm going to take a bath." Like, what is that about? They said: it says that I'm created b'tzelem Elokim — in the image of God. I have to take care of it. I have to take care of that.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'david-dancing',
    title: 'David Dancing Before the Ark',
    source: 'How Succot Teaches Happiness',
    audio: '/audio/dweck-sukkot/david-dancing.mp3',
    duration: '1:17',
    transcript: `And so too David, king of Israel, himself said — when he was dancing with the Ark, to bring the Ark to Jerusalem, and he was dancing to the point that he exposed himself a little bit more than a king should, wearing a simple tunic — and he went home. And his wife, who was the princess of Israel, because her father was Shaul HaMelech — Shaul, who wouldn't expose his wrist in public, that's how careful Shaul was — she looks down from the window, she sees the king dancing down there, and she says to him: "What an honor, what an honor this day is, to see the king of Israel dancing like one of the riffraff." Literally is what she says to him.

And he says to her: "I would bring myself even lower than that. Not like your father, who was thrown out of his throne in favor of someone other than him" — which happened to be David.

And it says: David danced with all of his might in front of HaKadosh Baruch Hu.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'sukkah-simplicity',
    title: 'The Simplicity of the Sukkah',
    source: 'How Succot Teaches Happiness',
    audio: '/audio/dweck-sukkot/sukkah-simplicity.mp3',
    duration: '0:50',
    transcript: `So that is a concept that I wanted to share for Sukkot, which is a very important part of Sukkot which we oftentimes miss. And the way that we celebrate it is so beautiful — because what do we do? We sit in a Sukkah. No major frills, right? Very minimalist Sukkah, no major frills. If we decorate things, what do we decorate with? We've got a few paper rings — but it's beautiful, it's just simple, it's just nice. We can go outside, when we're able to, and just accept that this is our life.

You take a piece of fruit and a leaf and a branch, and you hold the most simple things of the earth, and you raise them to HaKadosh Baruch Hu. What else do we do that? How beautiful that is.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'expectations',
    title: 'Expectations Are Premeditated Resentments',
    source: 'How Succot Teaches Happiness',
    audio: '/audio/dweck-sukkot/expectations.mp3',
    duration: '0:44',
    transcript: `So a nice term that they say in modern psychological circles is: expectations are premeditated resentments. Which is very accurate. What's it saying, essentially? The minute that you have an expectation, you already are ready for a resentment — because if the expectation doesn't work, then you resent the fact that it didn't work.

Now, what is that supposed to say — a person is not supposed to have expectations in life? Well, there's a difference between expectations and aspirations. Very big difference, right? Because expectations imply that this is expected. And the reality is that nothing is expected. Nothing.`,
  },
];
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <main class="dsp-page">
    <header class="dsp-hero">
      <p class="dsp-kicker">A few minutes each · Chag Sameach</p>
      <h1>Sukkot clips — pick your favorites</h1>
      <p class="dsp-lede">
        Pulled straight out of the shiurim. Listen to as many as you'd like, and tap the
        heart on the ones you like. It saves automatically — come back anytime, from any
        device, and your picks will still be here.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="dsp-clips">
      {
        clips.map((clip) => (
          <article class="dsp-clip" data-clip-id={clip.id} data-clip-title={clip.title}>
            <div class="dsp-clip-head">
              <div>
                <h2>{clip.title}</h2>
                <p class="dsp-clip-source">
                  {clip.source} · {clip.duration}
                </p>
              </div>
              <button type="button" class="dsp-like" aria-pressed="false" aria-label={`Like "${clip.title}"`}>
                <span class="dsp-like-icon" aria-hidden="true">♡</span>
                <span class="dsp-like-label">Like this one</span>
              </button>
            </div>

            <audio controls preload="none" src={clip.audio} />

            <details class="dsp-transcript">
              <summary>Transcript</summary>
              <p>{clip.transcript}</p>
            </details>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="dsp-status" id="dsp-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  .dsp-page {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 4rem;
    color: #e2e8f0;
  }
  .dsp-hero {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .dsp-kicker {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #7dd3fc;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .dsp-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 5vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .dsp-lede {
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: #94a3b8;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .dsp-clips {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .dsp-clip {
    border: 1px solid #1e293b;
    background: #0f172a;
    border-radius: 0.9rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem;
  }
  .dsp-clip-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }
  .dsp-clip h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f1f5f9;
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
  }
  .dsp-clip-source {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: #64748b;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .dsp-like {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border: 1px solid #334155;
    background: transparent;
    color: #cbd5e1;
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
  }
  .dsp-like:hover {
    border-color: #7dd3fc;
  }
  .dsp-like .dsp-like-icon {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .dsp-like[aria-pressed='true'] {
    border-color: #f472b6;
    background: rgba(244, 114, 182, 0.12);
    color: #fbcfe8;
  }
  .dsp-like[aria-pressed='true'] .dsp-like-icon {
    color: #f472b6;
  }
  .dsp-like[disabled] {
    opacity: 0.6;
    cursor: wait;
  }

  .dsp-clip audio {
    width: 100%;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }

  .dsp-transcript summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: #7dd3fc;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .dsp-transcript p {
    white-space: pre-line;
    color: #cbd5e1;
    line-height: 1.7;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .dsp-status {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    min-height: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: #7dd3fc;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const clips = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.dsp-clip');
  const status = document.getElementById('dsp-status');

  function setStatus(message: string) {
    if (status) status.textContent = message;
  }

  function applyLiked(button: HTMLButtonElement, liked: boolean) {
    button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(liked));
    const icon = button.querySelector('.dsp-like-icon');
    const label = button.querySelector('.dsp-like-label');
    if (icon) icon.textContent = liked ? '♥' : '♡';
    if (label) label.textContent = liked ? 'Liked' : 'Like this one';
  }

  async function loadPicks() {
    try {
      const res = await fetch('/api/sukkot-picks');
      if (!res.ok) return;
      const { liked } = await res.json();
      clips.forEach((clip) => {
        const id = clip.dataset.clipId!;
        const button = clip.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.dsp-like');
        if (button) applyLiked(button, Boolean(liked?.[id]));
      });
    } catch {
      // Picks just won't be pre-filled; liking still works going forward.
    }
  }

  clips.forEach((clip) => {
    const id = clip.dataset.clipId!;
    const title = clip.dataset.clipTitle ?? id;
    const button = clip.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.dsp-like');
    if (!button) return;

    button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const nextLiked = button.getAttribute('aria-pressed') !== 'true';
      applyLiked(button, nextLiked);
      button.disabled = true;
      setStatus('Saving…');
      try {
        const res = await fetch('/api/sukkot-picks', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify({ clip_id: id, title, liked: nextLiked }),
        });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error('save failed');
        setStatus('Saved.');
      } catch {
        applyLiked(button, !nextLiked);
        setStatus("Couldn't save that — try again in a moment.");
      } finally {
        button.disabled = false;
      }
    });
  });

  loadPicks();
</script>
